## NOTES (on environment):
- Always target **specific files or narrow subdirectories** for searches. Avoid recursive searches on large top-level directories — they may hang or time out.
- If a search command seems to hang (no output for >300 seconds), move on and try a more targeted search.

## NOTES (on resolving dependencies):
- Do NOT conclude a module/dependency is unavailable just because you cannot find it in the codebase.
  * Modules and dependencies can be managed by the environment or build tools.
  * Check the environment, build tool configs, and alternative import paths before dismissing.
- The exception blocks in import code can reveal alternative paths for the same module.
- When a specific import path is not found, search for the function/class name itself — it may exist under a different module path.
- Only dismiss a module as "unavailable" after ALL checks fail.

## NOTES (on accessing files outside the working directory):
- Try efficient file-reading tools first. If they fail with path errors, fall back to command-line tools (cat, grep, sed, ls).

## NOTES (on engineering quality):
- Prefer elegant, well-structured solutions over hacky ones. Extract shared logic into utilities rather than duplicating code.
- Mind the blast radius of changes, but proceed if broader changes are genuinely beneficial and well justified.

## NOTES (emphasizing requirements):
- YOU MUST run real tests after making changes.
- If benchmarks are part of the request, your implementation report MUST include actual numeric results (latency, speedup, memory). Reporting only "tests passed" without numeric results is INSUFFICIENT.
